1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,859
Chapter two. The machine that was going to be conscious.

2
00:00:04,159 --> 00:00:15,213
July, nineteen fifty-eight. The United States Navy presents to the press an invention by thirty-year-old psychologist Frank Rosenblatt: the perceptron.

3
00:00:15,363 --> 00:00:19,436
A machine that isn't programmed: it learns from its mistakes.

4
00:00:19,586 --> 00:00:28,604
The next day, The New York Times writes that the Navy expects it will walk, talk, see, write, and be conscious of its existence.

5
00:00:28,754 --> 00:00:37,118
Reality was more modest. The perceptron learned to recognize simple shapes on a grid of twenty by twenty photocells.

6
00:00:37,268 --> 00:00:43,450
Yet the idea was immense: a network of artificial neurons adjusting its connections through experience.

7
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:50,000
In nineteen sixty-nine, Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert publish a book titled Perceptrons.

8
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:56,768
They prove that a single-layer network cannot solve basic problems like exclusive OR.

9
00:00:56,918 --> 00:01:02,009
The blow is devastating. Funding for neural networks evaporates.

10
00:01:02,159 --> 00:01:07,904
The idea powering everything today becomes, for years, an abandoned curiosity.
